<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lex Manilia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">66 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lex Manilia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> proposed by the tribune Gaius Manilius in 66 BCE that gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> command of the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> and Tigranes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, with very wide powers in the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> spoke in its favour in his speech De imperio Cn. Pompei. Commands of this kind concentrated power in single generals and weakened the Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lex-manilia.jpg" alt="Lex Manilia" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Cristiano64 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roma_in_Oriente_66aC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war against Mithridates, commonly called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>, had been conducted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucius Licinius Lucullus</a>. By the winter of 68–67 BCE he had driven Mithridates out of Pontus and invaded the Armenian empire of his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a>. His advance stopped when his discontented troops mutinied under the leadership of Publius Clodius Pulcher; many of these Fimbrian legions had been in the East since the command of Gaius Flavius Fimbria in 86 BCE.</p><p>Mithridates and Tigranes took their chance. Mithridates invaded Pontus and Tigranes Cappadocia, and in the summer of 67 BCE Mithridates inflicted a crushing defeat on Roman forces under Lucullus's legate Triarius at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a>. Lucullus was superseded by the consul of 67 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manius-acilius-glabrio">Manius Acilius Glabrio</a>, who, seeing the difficulty of the situation, lingered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. By the end of that year Mithridates had recovered the whole of his former kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The law recalled the three commanders then in the East, Lucullus in Pontus, Glabrio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and Quintus Marcius Rex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, and transferred all their legions and the entire conduct of the eastern war to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. He was already in the East, completing the command against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> given him by the lex Gabinia of 67 BCE.</p><p>That earlier law had been opposed by almost the whole Senate. The proposal of Manilius, by contrast, had the support of many senators and of several eminent former consuls, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-servilius-vatia-isauricus">Publius Servilius Vatia Isauricus</a>, Gaius Scribonius Curio, Gaius Cassius Longinus Varus and Gnaeus Cornelius Lentulus Clodianus. Cicero was serving as praetor when he delivered his speech for it, which survives. Opposition was largely confined to Quintus Hortensius and Quintus Lutatius Catulus, whose arguments against giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> the command Cicero answers at length.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The law was passed in the Comitia Tributa without the violence that had attended the proposal of Gabinius the year before. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> soon moved against Mithridates and Tigranes and had defeated both by the end of 65 BCE, though Mithridates was not killed until 63 BCE. Manilius himself was prosecuted twice after leaving office in December 66 BCE. On the first occasion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> defended him against a charge of extortion, and the case was dropped in January 65 BCE amid disturbances and violence. On the second he was condemned on a charge of maiestas and went into exile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lex Manilia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…gave Sulla the command and the army that he turned against Rome itself, and the Third made the careers of Lucullus and Pompey; the extraordinary command granted to Pompey under the <b>Lex Manilia</b> in 66 BCE was a milestone in the concentration of power that ended the Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
